safari.patch - the browser build: translated code without the two stalls measured in
docs/issues/browser-safari.md. For "patch -p1" in the Play! tree, AFTER play-net.patch,
play-web.patch and perf.patch (it changes lines that perf.patch wrote). Made from
local/play-web-safari/base-src (the tree tools/play-web/build.sh makes from the patches
as they were on 2026-10-13 10:52, play-net.patch of commit 356add2) and work-src, by
local/play-web-safari/out/mkpatch.sh.

What it changes (three files of Play!'s core, one of its code generator; nothing of the
page, of tools/play-web/src or of the game):

  deps/CodeGen/src/MemoryFunction.cpp
      A block of translated code gets its place in the program's function table from a
      run of 4,096 places made by one growth of the table, instead of one growth per
      block (Emscripten's addFunction). In Safari's engine growing a table costs time
      for every instance that imports it - since perf.patch that is every block - so a
      place cost 1.3 to 3 ms once there were thousands of blocks. Places given back are
      used again first, as before.
  Source/ee/VuExecutor.cpp, VuExecutor.h
      The blocks made for a VU1 program are kept when VU1's program memory changes, and
      taken up again when the same content comes back: one executor per content of the
      whole of program memory (at most 64, the one unused longest goes). perf.patch
      threw every VU1 block away at every change, which a level that alternates
      between programs does many times a frame.
  Source/GenericMipsExecutor.h
      The handler for "no block here" is installed by a method of its own, so that the
      executor in use can install its own (needed by the above; no change for the main
      processor and the IOP).

To merge:
  1. tools/play-web/build.sh: one line after  apply perf "$HERE/perf.patch" :
         apply safari "$HERE/safari.patch"
     and the patch named in the script's header comment and in the licence note's list
     of patches (README.txt in the site's licences folder), as perf.patch is.
  2. A tree that has the three patches takes this one as it is (build.sh applies it and
     compiles what changed: about 60 files include GenericMipsExecutor.h). Nothing else
     has to be built from scratch and no option changes.
  3. If perf.patch's sections for MemoryFunction.cpp or VuExecutor.cpp/.h are written
     again, this patch has to be made again on top (mkpatch.sh); play-net.patch and
     play-web.patch do not touch these files.
  4. Check after merging: the page boots; "blocks" in rta.info() (functions) stops
     rising while standing in a level; in Safari the first level comes up without
     seconds of stillness.

